          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-c648bd2e9a7ad647\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":3,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Emma strongly avows as true her consuming love for Rodolphe. The narrator also characterizes such language as the soul’s fullness sometimes “overflowing” through conventional metaphors, giving the words independently supported expressive/discharge force. The highlighted avowal does not itself undertake a commitment.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The habitual, conventional form could be treated as merely an impassioned T avowal, but the narrator’s explicit overflow account supplies substantial positive evidence for E; its deliberateness and elaboration keep E below maximal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Emma’s direct words are narratively revoiced as part of a recurrent, habitual exchange, marked by the imperfect “reprenait-elle” (“she would resume”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Emma resumes this declaration while weeping and seeking reassurance about Rodolphe’s fidelity. Her ensuing claims of dependence, jealousy, submission, and adoration amplify a sincere but possessive and idealizing passion. Rodolphe discounts the conventional language because previous lovers used similar phrases, but the narrator explicitly distinguishes similarity of expression from difference of feeling and presents Emma’s language as an imperfect overflow of genuine emotional fullness. His skeptical and exploitative reception therefore does not reduce the utterance’s T or E force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oh! c’est que je t’aime! … je t’aime à ne pouvoir me passer de toi”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal and claim that she cannot live without him strongly support T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Emma describes being torn by “toutes les colères de l’amour,” fears other women, and declares Rodolphe her king and idol.\",\"supports\":\"Her surrounding speech presents intense, consuming affect that corroborates the avowed loving state, while not by itself establishing E or P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says that “la plénitude de l’âme ne débordait pas quelquefois par les métaphores les plus vides.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit characterization of emotional fullness overflowing into words provides independent evidence that the utterance functions partly as expressive discharge, supporting E.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rodolphe hears these declarations as familiar and unoriginal, but the narrator says he fails to distinguish dissimilar feelings beneath similar expressions.\",\"supports\":\"The conventionality of the wording does not negate Emma’s sincere avowal or its expressive force; Rodolphe’s skepticism concerns interpretation rather than core ontology.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“reprenait-elle” and the surrounding imperfect-tense account present this as what Emma repeatedly would say.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes the utterance as narratively quoted or revoiced within a habitual scene rather than a uniquely situated single speech event.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal and E captures the narrator-supported overflow of affect into conventional language. The utterance itself contains no important residual force requiring O, and its surrounding jealousy, idealization, and Rodolphe’s exploitation are contextual rather than missing core categories.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
